# The national unit for usa. Items take n=1; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g3-usa-our-country-long-ago v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=9 subject=history nation=usa levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The first towns of North America"
  goal "Put events of the land that is now the United States in order, and say how we know about peoples who left no writing."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1000 to=2000 step=200 years=[1050, 1903, 1969] labels=["Cahokia", "First flight", "Moon landing"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Long before any European came, Native American peoples farmed and built towns. At Cahokia, near the Mississippi River, a town of great earth mounds grew from about AD 1050 and lasted until about 1350. At Mesa Verde, the Ancestral Puebloans built houses in the cliffs, and the last families left about 1285. Much later, in 1903, the first aeroplane flew in North Carolina, and in 1969 Americans landed on the Moon.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n3-what n=1
    }
    show history.n3-order n=1 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n3-between n=1 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n3-century n=1 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.across-year-one k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.which-century k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n3-order n=1 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n3-what n=1
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n3-century n=1 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.bc-ad-order k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n3-between n=1 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n3-why n=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n3-find-out n=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Every country's story goes back further than its writing. Where there is no writing, the ground and the buildings tell it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to put the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, evidence without
    writing) to work on the family's own country. The way in names what the place or thing was;
    the core orders three events of the country, counts the years between two, names a century,
    and reviews three of the grade's timeline questions; the two-star question asks how we know.
    The wrong idea to watch for is that a date in a book is when something began, when it is often
    only the first time it was written down or the oldest thing found. If the child is stuck, put
    the three events on a line you draw together. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you, near home. Standards for the third grade in many states, such as California's, ask children to learn about the American Indian nations of their region and how their community has changed.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the place was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions stay on one side of year one."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap in it, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
